INT. MUNICIPAL WATERWORKS PUMP HOUSE - NIGHT

A cathedral of riveted iron and brass, three stories high, lit by one amber work lamp. Steam pipes sweat along the walls. The great pump wheel turns overhead with a patient, heavy rhythm, and behind a brick archway, a vault door the color of old pennies.

SILAS "THE ACCOUNTANT" BRANNIGAN, fifties, silver hair, wire spectacles, wears a gray overcoat and a face like a closed ledger. He kneels at the vault, ear to the steel.

LORETTA "LOLLY" DUCHAMP, forties, a redhead in a mechanic's coverall, holds a stopwatch and a flashlight.

MARCUS JEROME FALK, thirties, tall and broad, wears a night watchman's cap two sizes too small, and sweats through it.

NELL PETRAKIS, twenties, sits on a crate with a tool roll across her knees, pale and unblinking.

SILAS
Four minutes before the watchman's round.

LOLLY
Three and a half. He's early on Thursdays.

MARCUS
He's early because the Missus makes him.

SILAS
Hush. Nell, the drill.

Nell unrolls the tools, hands him a slim steel bit. He nods once. The pump wheel overhead thumps like a heart.

LOLLY
(whispering)
Nobody breathe.

Silas turns the bit. The vault ticks.
